WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
All right.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:07.350
Let's get into some perfect audience questions
for the week, very election relevant.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.270
The first one is David.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:14.150
Can you explain, is the Electoral College
good or bad?

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.520
Because I hear different people saying different
things.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.200
Well, it should come as no.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.720
As no surprise that different people have
different opinions about the Electoral College.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.020
But in order to answer the question, is the
Electoral College good or bad, you have to

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.390
first understand what the point of the Electoral
College is.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:33.559
And number two, what one considers good or
bad may depend on where they sit politically.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:37.760
So let's kind of build up our understanding
of this in the United States.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.309
If a hundred million people vote 55 million
vote for one candidate, 45 million vote for

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:49.760
another candidate, it is not guaranteed that
the one with the 55 million votes won.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.159
Why is that?

00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:53.350
Because we don't have a national popular vote.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:58.629
We have an electoral college system where
each state is worth a certain number of votes

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:05.640
or points, as they were the first to get to
270 points or electoral votes wins.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.920
So you could win some states by crazy, huge
popular vote margins, win the popular vote,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.910
but only have 265 electoral votes.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:24.130
A different candidate could have fewer total
votes, but win enough electoral votes to win.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:31.070
And in fact, that is what happened in the
year 2000 with George W. Bush and Al Gore.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.450
And it is what happened in did it happen in
2004?

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:39.450
It obviously happened recently with Donald
Trump in in 2016.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:47.140
So what we see is that the Electoral College
gives disproportionately more or less power

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.300
to certain states in deciding the outcome
of the election.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.480
And by virtue of doing that, the voters in
those states have relatively more or less

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:04.420
power in total because a vote in California
is not equally likely to decide the outcome,

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.900
as is a vote in, for example, Pennsylvania
or Florida.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:08.900
Good.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.479
Why do we have this system?

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:17.060
Initially, the idea of the Electoral College
was, listen, if candidates if we have a national

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:23.219
popular vote, candidates will spend all their
time in the most populated areas and California

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:25.879
and New York will decide the election.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:30.430
That's where candidates will go and they will
ignore the small and rural states.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:36.959
Well, all you've really done with the Electoral
College system is change that around.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:42.840
So that now the states that decide the election
are a handful of so-called battleground states,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:49.180
including but not limited to Florida, Wisconsin,
Pennsylvania, Michigan, North Carolina, Arizona,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.180
etc..

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.930
So in particular in 2020 when.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.819
Listen, I don't need the candidate coming
to where I am.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:00.189
We have enough media.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:04.090
We have enough Internet where I can make a
completely informed decision.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.359
I don't care if the candidate never goes on
the campaign trail at all.

00:03:07.359 --> 00:03:12.439
Quite frankly, if I can understand policy
differences and sort of understand who who

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:13.590
the candidates are.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.680
So I do believe that the Electoral College
is outdated first and foremost.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:24.779
I do believe the Electoral College has created
the opposite of the problem they wanted to

00:03:24.779 --> 00:03:30.920
solve by basically making it so candidates
are ignoring the very populated areas.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:37.120
But most importantly, when you say is it good
or bad, would I go to is whether the Electoral

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.989
College better reflects the will of the people
than a national popular vote.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:44.370
And the answer is no.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:50.670
More than two million more people voted for
Hillary Clinton than for Donald Trump in 2016.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.049
Now, you could say, well, if it was a national
popular vote, the election would have been

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:57.319
run differently, run a differently than as
a candidate.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:03:58.319
Fine.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:00.549
But way more people wanted Hillary.

00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:02.620
And yet Donald Trump is president.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:10.080
And fundamentally, the Electoral College system
is one of the many systems that gives a structural

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.389
advantage to Republicans.

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:16.259
It is Republicans who have benefited from
having their candidate win despite losing

00:04:16.259 --> 00:04:18.260
the popular vote multiple times now.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:25.560
It is Republicans who benefit from the sort
of baseline reality that conservatives are

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.100
more likely to live in rural areas than are
liberals merely because of preferences.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:37.290
And our system disproportionately gives power
to rural states when it comes to the Electoral

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.400
College.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.840
And importantly, in the Senate, the Senate
is another way in which our government is

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.340
stacked against the left.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.030
Each state gets two senators.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:53.030
The highest population states tend to be blue
leaning states, left leaning states.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.229
And so you have a situation.

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:59.770
I'm thinking back to the election where something
like the senators represent.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:05.870
Only 17 percent of the population have half
of the votes in the Senate.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:06.870
That's crazy.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.280
The Senate is fundamentally undemocratic.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:17.190
So to step back, I do believe the Electoral
College is a negative influence on the will

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.949
of the people being expressed in who becomes
president of the United States.

00:05:21.949 --> 00:05:24.889
I do believe that it should be changed.

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:31.009
And when you look at some of the data, the
10 least populated states plus D.C. have a

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:36.080
population that's three percent of the country
and they have almost seven percent of the

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.080
electors.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.760
They are more than do X overrepresented.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.409
And we can slice and dice it in other ways
to give these kind of notable examples.

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:46.740
But I believe the Electoral College has to
go.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:51.349
I think it is a bad influence on the United
States system of picking presidents.

00:05:51.349 --> 00:05:53.730
If you disagree with me, let me know.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:06:14.789
I would love to hear 

00:06:14.789 --> 00:07:51.199
from you.

